<p>I’ve always been a fan of musical styles that are formed when people from different traditions interact. I’m sceptical that any musical tradition is ‘pure’ in that way, because people have alwayse met, listened to and been inspired by each others’ musical styles and traditions, so you can hear subtle or more overt influences in most styles of music. However, I think it’s really exciting when musical traditions that seem very disparate are merged. It seems as if it can’t possibly work, but what results is something unique and exciting. Ethiojazz (or Ethio-Jazz, or Ethio Jazz: I’ve seen it written in many different ways) is a great example of this.</p><p>I <a href="https://www.rousette.org.uk/archives/recent-music/">first came across Ethiojazz</a> through the work of the <a href="https://elen-music.com/portfolio/samuel-yirga/">Ethiopian pianist Samuel Yirga</a>. I have enjoyed listening to his albums for many years, but for some reason, I had never looked into the origins of Ethiojazz or sought out other artists playing it. I finally put that right a couple of months ago. Apparently, Ethiojazz originated in the 1950s when Armenian refugees who had settled in Ethiopia started to blend Armenian jazz with traditional Ethiopian folk music, later incorporating elements of funk, Latin American rhythms, soul, and any other elements that its practitioners thought were cool. The ‘seems as if it shouldn’t work’ element in this case is that Ethiopian Amharic folk music uses the pentatonic (five-tone) scale, while Western music uses the 12-tone scale. Listening to music combining the two makes your brain fizz in a very enjoyable way. When you add in a lot of different rhythms from a bunch of musical styles, all overlaid on top of each other, it makes irresistibly compelling and intriguing music. Your brain is trying to pick apart which musical scale and rhythm comes from where, but your hips are yelling “WHO CARES!! LET’S SHIMMY!”. I guarantee that if you listen to Ethiojazz you will find yourself (at least) doing a bit of chair dancing, if not throwing some extraordinary shapes across the room.</p><p>I’ve been listening a lot to the music of Mulatu Astatke, who is one of the early pioneers of the style. There’s a fantastic compilation album called ‘Ethiopiques, Vol. 4: Ethio Jazz &amp; Musique Instrumentale (1969-1974)’ — catchy title, I know. It gathers together Mulatu Astatke’s best compositions from the period, and is a great introduction to Ethiojazz. The songs have irresistible melodies, slinky, funky rhythms, and I can’t get enough of them. I love the tracks ‘Yègellé Tezeta (My Own Memory)’ and ‘Yèkatit (February)’, but I think my favourite is ‘Yèkèrmo Sèw (A Man of Wisdom and Experience)’. Samuel Yirga’s music is also a fantastic choice if you want to hear a modern interpretation of the Ethiojazz tradition.</p><p>Despite what some people try to claim, I think diversity always makes our lives richer and more interesting. Ethiojazz is a great example of that.</p>